  <item>
    <title><![CDATA[HDPE 100 Pipe Scrap]]></title>
    <link>https://alqaryangroup.com/materials-we-deal/hdpe-100-pipe-scrap/</link>
    <pubDate>Sun, 01 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
    <dc:creator><![CDATA[qaryan_admin]]></dc:creator>
    <guid isPermaLink="false">https://alqaryangroup.com/?page_id=1041</guid>
    <description></description>
    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:html -->

<header class="q-site-head">
  <div class="q-site-head__inner">
    <a class="q-brand" href="/">
      <span class="q-brand__mark">AQ</span>
      <span class="q-brand__name">Al Qaryan <em>Industries</em></span>
    </a>
    <nav class="q-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href="/about/">About</a>
      <a href="/services/">Services</a>
      <a href="/materials-we-deal/">Materials We Deal</a>
      <a href="/we-buy/">We Buy Scrap</a>
      <a href="/facility/">Facility</a>
      <a href="/quality/">Quality</a>
      <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>
      <a class="q-nav__cta" href="/contact/">Contact</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
<div class="q-hero q-hero--compact">
  <p class="q-kicker">Materials we deal · HDPE pipe scrap</p>
  <h1>HDPE 100 pipe scrap</h1>
  <p class="q-lead">HDPE 100 pipe scrap is the residue of PE100 pressure pipe — offcuts, rejected lengths, decommissioned mains and spool ends collected from water, gas and telecom projects. PE100 is the highest grade in the polyethylene pipe family, and its scrap is one of the most sought-after polyolefin streams in Pakistan because the base resin starts life clean, heavy and fully characterised. Al Qaryan Industries buys, sorts and recycles HDPE 100 pipe scrap at our Shershah Industrial Area facility in Karachi and supplies the recovered material to processors across the country.</p>
  <div class="q-chips"><span class="q-chip">PE100 · MRS 10 MPa</span><span class="q-chip">Blue, black, yellow, orange</span><span class="q-chip">Shredded, regrind or bales</span><span class="q-chip">Washed and dried</span></div>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>What HDPE 100 pipe scrap actually is</h2>
  <p>PE100 is a bimodal high-density polyethylene classified by its minimum required strength of 10 MPa at 20 degrees Celsius for fifty years, the definition set out in ISO 12162. That strength comes from the molecular architecture rather than from additives, which is why a PE100 pipe that has spent twenty years underground still contains a genuinely high-value polymer. When a municipal water line is replaced, when a gas distribution network is upgraded, or when a contractor finishes a project with unused metreage, the material that comes back is HDPE 100 pipe scrap.</p>
  <p>The scrap arrives in very different shapes: whole coils of small diameter pipe, cut lengths of 90 mm to 630 mm mains, flanged spools from pump stations, fusion-joint offcuts, and the bulky 800 mm and 1000 mm sections recovered from irrigation and outfall schemes. Each shape needs different handling. Coiled material is easy to bale and cheap to transport; large diameter sections are mostly air and have to be cut or crushed on site before they are worth moving.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>The HDPE 100 pipe scrap grades we handle</h2>
  <p>Pipe scrap is not one commodity. Colour tells you the application, and the application tells you what else is likely to be inside the load. We separate before we buy, because a mixed load of blue water pipe and orange duct is worth less to everybody than two clean loads.</p>
  <table class="q-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Blue PE100</th><td>Potable water mains. Usually the cleanest stream and the easiest to resell as regrind.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Yellow or black-yellow PE100</th><td>Gas distribution pipe. Requires a documented source; we do not accept unknown-origin gas pipe.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Orange PE100</th><td>Telecom and power duct. Often thin-walled, frequently mixed with draw rope and couplers.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Black PE100 with co-extruded stripes</th><td>General pressure pipe. Stripe layers are the same polymer family and do not affect regrind.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>PE100-RC</th><td>Crack-resistant grade used under roads. Treated as PE100 in recycling terms.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Large diameter sections</th><td>300 mm and above, including 630 mm, 800 mm and 1000 mm outfall pipe. Needs cutting before transport.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Fusion joint offcuts and spool ends</th><td>Short lengths and flanged pieces from fabrication yards.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="q-note">PE100, PE80 and PE63 are not interchangeable on a pressure application, but once recycled none of them keeps a pressure rating — see the processing section below.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Where our HDPE 100 pipe scrap comes from</h2>
  <p>Most of our PE100 pipe scrap originates from infrastructure work: water supply schemes, gas distribution contractors, telecom ducting projects and irrigation lining programmes. We buy from contractors clearing a completed site, from utility depots disposing of surplus stock, and from scrap traders who aggregate smaller lots from many sites. Alongside that we import baled and shredded HDPE pipe from the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Malaysia and Indonesia, where large-diameter PE100 mains are replaced on a scheduled cycle and the material is already segregated at source.</p>
  <p>Because pipe scrap is heavy and bulky at the same time, distance matters more than it does for film or bottles. That is why we concentrate on Karachi and the surrounding industrial belt for local purchases, and only import when the landed cost of clean, already-shredded material beats what we can collect ourselves.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Specification we work to</h2>
  <p>PE100 resin itself has a well-known envelope: density in the region of 0.945 to 0.960 g/cm3 and a very low melt flow index, because pipe resin is deliberately high in molecular weight. Recycled pipe scrap sits close to that envelope for density, while melt flow moves with the history of the material and with how much it has been reprocessed. We therefore confirm every figure on the lot rather than quote a catalogue number.</p>
  <table class="q-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Polymer</th><td>High-density polyethylene, PE100 classification</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Minimum required strength</th><td>MRS 10 MPa at 20 °C, 50 years (ISO 12162)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Typical density</th><td>0.945 to 0.960 g/cm3 for the virgin resin; measured per lot on regrind</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Melt flow index</th><td>Low, pipe-grade. Exact figure depends on lot history and is reported after testing</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Colour</th><td>Blue, black, yellow, orange, or mixed on request</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Form supplied</th><td>Shredded, regrind, agglomerate or pellets; baled on request</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Moisture</th><td>Checked before dispatch; dried where the buyer process requires it</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Contamination</th><td>Soil, concrete, metal and non-PE plastics removed by hand and by density separation</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Packing</th><td>25 kg bags, jumbo bags, or loose in bulk</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Documents</th><td>Batch test report on request; third-party inspection such as SGS available</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Contamination that decides the value of a load</h2>
  <p>The single biggest destroyer of PE100 pipe scrap value is not dirt, it is the wrong polymer hiding in the load. Cross-linked polyethylene — PEX pipe from plumbing — looks like PE, feels like PE and cannot be melted back into a usable product because its molecular network is permanent. A small percentage of PEX in a load shows up as gel particles and hard specks in the finished part, and no amount of washing removes it. We screen for PEX by source and by hand at intake.</p>
  <p>After that come the mechanical contaminants. Sand and soil from trench work abrade the shredder and carry into the wash water. Concrete collars and mortar stuck to the pipe wall are removed by knocking and tumbling before shredding. Steel reinforcement, flange plates, bolts and couplers are picked up by magnet and by metal detector. Draw rope, tape and cable pulled through telecom duct has to be cut out by hand, and pipe ends with solvent-cement transitions or threaded brass inserts are trimmed off and rejected.</p>
  <p>Water is the last one, and it is easy to underestimate. Pipe that has been stored outdoors holds water in the bore, and shredding it produces wet flake that will not feed a pelletiser properly. We dry to a measured moisture level and report it, because a buyer who receives damp regrind blames the material for what is really a drying problem.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>How we collect HDPE 100 pipe scrap</h2>
  <p>For contractors we work on a site-clearance basis: we inspect the material where it lies, agree a rate on the assessed grade, and bring the cutting and loading equipment needed to reduce large diameter pipe to transportable lengths. For traders and depots we buy by weight after inspection at our yard, with a written record of the grade agreed so there is no argument later about what was in the truck.</p>
  <p>Suppliers who bring us clean, single-colour, single-application HDPE 100 pipe scrap consistently get the best rate, because that material goes straight into our own production without a sorting stage. Mixed loads are still bought, but they are priced as mixed loads and processed as such.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>How we process and recycle PE100 pipe scrap</h2>
  <p>The route is shredding, washing, drying, and then either agglomeration or pelletising depending on what the customer can feed. Large diameter pipe goes through a heavy-duty shredder first because it will not fit any granulator; smaller pipe and offcuts go straight to the granulator. Washing removes soil and residual water-borne deposits, and a density stage separates any polypropylene couplers or polyethylene of a different grade that slipped through. Drying brings moisture down to a level the pelletiser accepts, and the pelletiser with its filtration screen takes out the last fine contamination.</p>
  <p>One point of honesty that matters commercially: recycled PE100 is not PE100. The pressure rating of a pipe is a property of the whole extrusion, not of the resin bag, and reprocessing shortens the molecular chains enough that the material cannot be certified back to MRS 10 MPa. What you get from recycled PE100 pipe scrap is a high-quality, high-density, low-flow polyethylene that is excellent for non-pressure products — and we say so plainly rather than let a buyer discover it in a burst test.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>What is made from recycled HDPE 100 pipe scrap</h2>
  <p>The recovered material goes overwhelmingly into non-pressure applications where stiffness, chemical resistance and impact strength matter but hoop stress does not.</p>
  <ul class="q-list">
    <li>Non-pressure drainage and sewer pipe, including double-wall and corrugated structures</li>
    <li>Telecom duct and cable protection pipe, where orange or black colour is acceptable</li>
    <li>Cable spacers, duct couplers and underground cable covers</li>
    <li>Manhole covers, chambers and inspection boxes</li>
    <li>HDPE sheets and boards for lining, cutting boards and marine fendering</li>
    <li>Rotomoulded water tanks, bins and road barriers, where PE100 regrind blends well with rotomoulding grades</li>
    <li>Injection-moulded crates, pallets, and industrial containers</li>
    <li>Blow-moulded drums for non-critical contents</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="q-note">If your product needs a certified pressure rating, tell us at enquiry stage — we will say clearly whether recycled material can meet it or whether you need virgin resin.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>What moves the price of HDPE 100 pipe scrap</h2>
    <p>PE100 pipe scrap does not trade like bottle scrap. Its price follows three things at once: the virgin HDPE price it competes with, the pace of municipal and utility work in Sindh and Punjab, and the cost of moving something that is mostly air. When civil works slow — in the monsoon months, or when a provincial budget is delayed — the material piles up at contractors' yards and the price softens. When two large water schemes are running at once, the opposite happens and buyers compete for the same tonnes.</p>
    <p>The second variable is grade purity. A load that is single-colour, single-application and free of PEX commands a genuine premium, because it goes straight into production with no sorting cost. A mixed load of blue water pipe, orange duct and black gas pipe is still saleable, but it is priced as a mixed stream and processed with a sorting stage attached. Sellers who separate before they call us consistently get the better number, and that is not a negotiating tactic — it reflects a real difference in what we can do with the material.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Specifying HDPE 100 pipe scrap: what to send us</h2>
  <ul class="q-list">
    <li>Diameter range and approximate wall thickness — 63 mm to 630 mm is our normal window, and larger sections need cutting before transport</li>
    <li>Colour mix and, if known, the application each colour came from (water, gas, telecom duct)</li>
    <li>The form you can supply: whole lengths, cut sections, shredded, or already baled</li>
    <li>Approximate tonnage and whether it is a one-off clearance or a recurring monthly volume</li>
    <li>Any known contamination — concrete collars, steel fittings, draw rope, or pipe that is still full of water</li>
    <li>Location, access for a truck, and whether loading equipment is available on site</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Buying HDPE 100 pipe scrap from Al Qaryan Industries</h2>
    <p>Al Qaryan Industries has been buying and selling HDPE 100 pipe scrap since 2007 and handles around 500 tons of plastic a month across all grades. We buy from contractors, utilities, fabricators and traders, and we sell shredded and reground PE100 pipe scrap to pipe makers, duct manufacturers, moulders and compounders throughout Pakistan.</p>
    <p>If you are buying HDPE 100 pipe scrap from us, send the application, the form you can handle, the colour you can accept, and the monthly tonnage. We will confirm what is in stock, what is in process, and what is coming in from imports, then send a price against your quantity. Trial lots are welcome and we would rather you run one than take a container on trust.</p>
    <p>If you are selling HDPE 100 pipe scrap to us, send photographs of the material, the approximate tonnage, the diameter range and the location. For large diameter or site-clearance lots we will come and look. We are reachable on WhatsApp at +92 333 201 0506 for a quick assessment before anything moves.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Questions buyers and suppliers ask</h2>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Can recycled PE100 still be used for pressure pipe?</summary><p>No, and any supplier who tells you otherwise is guessing. Reprocessing changes the molecular weight distribution, so the material cannot be certified back to MRS 10 MPa. Recycled PE100 pipe scrap is used for non-pressure pipe, duct, sheet and moulded products where it performs very well.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Do you buy pipe with concrete or soil still attached?</summary><p>Yes, but it is priced accordingly because cleaning costs us handling time and wear. Knocking off loose concrete and cutting out metal fittings before you deliver moves the load into a better grade and a better rate.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>What is the difference between PE100, PE80 and PE63 scrap?</summary><p>They are the same polymer family with different minimum required strengths: 10 MPa, 8 MPa and 6.3 MPa respectively. For recycling they behave similarly, but we keep them separate so a buyer who needs a specific density and stiffness gets a consistent feed.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>How is the material packed for delivery?</summary><p>Shredded or reground material goes in 25 kg bags or jumbo bags; bulky pipe can be delivered loose by the truckload. Deliveries move by road anywhere in Pakistan.</p></details>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Related materials</h2>
  <div class="q-grid q-grid--3">
  <div class="q-card"><h3>HDPE 80 scrap</h3><p>The mid-strength grade from older water and irrigation networks.</p><a class="q-link" href="/materials-we-deal/hdpe-80-scrap/">HDPE 80 scrap</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>HDPE 60 scrap</h3><p>Legacy PE63 pipe recovered from decommissioned lines.</p><a class="q-link" href="/materials-we-deal/hdpe-60-scrap/">HDPE 60 scrap</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>PVC pipe scrap</h3><p>uPVC drainage and sewer pipe recovered for regrind and compound.</p><a class="q-link" href="/materials-we-deal/pvc-pipe-scrap/">PVC pipe scrap</a></div>
  </div>
</div>


<div class="q-cta">
  <div class="q-cta__text">
    <strong>Buying this material, or selling it to us?</strong>
    <span>Send the grade, the quantity and your city — we reply with availability and price.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="q-cta__actions">
    <a class="q-btn q-btn--primary" href="/contact/">Request a quote</a>
