</script>

<script type="application/ld+json">
{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can recycled polycarbonate not be used for water bottles again?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because food-contact reuse requires a certified decontamination process and a documented BPA position that recycled bottle regrind cannot support. Recycled PC goes into industrial applications instead, where it performs extremely well."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the regrind need drying?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, always. Polycarbonate absorbs moisture and hydrolyses when it is melted wet, which produces brittle parts. We dry and report moisture, but the buyer should still dry according to their own process."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you separate clear from coloured PC?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, when the volume justifies it. Clear and light blue material is kept separate because it retains more end-use options."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does PC compare with ABS for recycling?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"PC is tougher and more transparent; ABS is easier to mould and more available. Both are engineering plastics and both are kept out of the commodity polyolefin streams."}}]}
</script>

