    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:html -->

<header class="q-site-head">
  <div class="q-site-head__inner">
    <a class="q-brand" href="/">
      <span class="q-brand__mark">AQ</span>
      <span class="q-brand__name">Al Qaryan <em>Industries</em></span>
    </a>
    <nav class="q-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href="/about/">About</a>
      <a href="/services/">Services</a>
      <a href="/materials-we-deal/">Materials We Deal</a>
      <a href="/we-buy/">We Buy Scrap</a>
      <a href="/facility/">Facility</a>
      <a href="/quality/">Quality</a>
      <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>
      <a class="q-nav__cta" href="/contact/">Contact</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
<div class="q-hero q-hero--compact">
  <p class="q-kicker">BUYING GUIDE · PVC AND HDPE</p>
  <h1>PVC and HDPE Pipe Sellers in Pakistan — How to Choose One</h1>
  <p class="q-lead">Most pipe problems in Pakistan are not manufacturing failures; they are buying mistakes — the wrong pressure class, an unlicensed seller, or a quote that leaves out freight and fittings. This page is the checklist we would want a buyer to run before ordering from anyone, including us.</p>
  <div class="q-chips"><span class="q-chip">Manufacturer vs dealer</span><span class="q-chip">PSQCA licence</span><span class="q-chip">SDR / PN explained</span><span class="q-chip">Quote checklist</span><span class="q-chip">Karachi since 2007</span></div>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Seller, dealer, manufacturer — why the word matters</h2>
  <p>In Pakistan the words "seller", "dealer", "supplier" and "manufacturer" are used almost interchangeably in listings, but they are not the same business. A <strong>manufacturer</strong> extrudes the pipe and owns the formulation and the wall thickness. A <strong>dealer or distributor</strong> resells someone else's pipe, often without knowing which plant made which length. A <strong>trader</strong> may hold no stock at all and buy against your order.</p>
  <p>None of these is dishonest, but they answer different questions. A dealer can be the right choice for a small mixed order from stock. A manufacturer is the right choice when you need a specific pressure class, a consistent monthly programme, or someone accountable when a batch is out of specification.</p>
  <p>The practical test is one question: <strong>"Which plant made this pipe, and will you name it in writing?"</strong> A manufacturer answers immediately. A dealer who cannot answer is telling you what your recourse will look like if something goes wrong.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Check the PSQCA licence, not the brochure</h2>
  <p>Pipe sold in Pakistan against a Pakistan Standard carries a PSQCA licence, and the licence number is tied to a specific product and a specific plant. Ask for the licence number and check it, rather than accepting a logo printed on a catalogue cover.</p>
  <p>The same applies to quality management certification. ISO 9001 covers the plant's process control, not the pipe in your hand. A certificate is a starting point for a conversation, never a substitute for a test report against the standard you specified.</p>
  <ul class="q-list">
    <li>Ask for the <strong>PSQCA licence number</strong> and the plant address it belongs to — they should match.</li>
    <li>Ask which <strong>standard</strong> the pipe is made to: ISO 1452 or ASTM D1785/D2241 for PVC-U, ISO 4427 or DIN 8074/8075 for PE100.</li>
    <li>Ask for a <strong>batch test report</strong>: wall thickness, outside diameter and hydrostatic test result for the lot you are buying.</li>
    <li>Ask whether the pipe will be <strong>marked</strong> with the size, pressure class, standard and manufacturer — unmarked pipe cannot be traced.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Get the pressure class right before you compare prices</h2>
  <p>A price per metre is meaningless without the pressure class attached to it. The same outside diameter in the same material can carry very different pressures depending on wall thickness, and the cheap quote is usually the thinner wall.</p>
  <p>For HDPE the class is set by SDR — standard dimension ratio. A lower SDR means a thicker wall and a higher pressure rating, so <strong>SDR 17 in PE100 is PN 10 while SDR 11 in the same resin is PN 16</strong>. For PVC-U the equivalent classes run PN 6, PN 10, PN 12.5 and PN 16, and Schedule 40 and Schedule 80 describe wall thickness rather than pressure directly.</p>
  <p>If two quotes differ sharply, compare the SDR or Schedule lines before the totals. A difference of one SDR step is often the whole gap.</p>
  <table class="q-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>HDPE PE100 — SDR 26</th><td>PN 6</td></tr>
      <tr><th>HDPE PE100 — SDR 21</th><td>PN 8</td></tr>
      <tr><th>HDPE PE100 — SDR 17</th><td>PN 10</td></tr>
      <tr><th>HDPE PE100 — SDR 13.6</th><td>PN 12.5</td></tr>
      <tr><th>HDPE PE100 — SDR 11</th><td>PN 16</td></tr>
      <tr><th>PVC-U — SDR 41</th><td>PN 6</td></tr>
      <tr><th>PVC-U — SDR 26</th><td>PN 10</td></tr>
      <tr><th>PVC-U — SDR 21</th><td>PN 12.5</td></tr>
      <tr><th>PVC-U — SDR 17</th><td>PN 16</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="q-note">These are standard pressure classes at 20 °C for water. Both materials lose pressure capability as temperature rises, so a line running warm must be derated against the standard you are specifying.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>The six things a pipe quote must state</h2>
  <p>A quote that gives only a rate per metre cannot be compared with another one. Ask every seller to put the same six lines in writing — the ones who cannot are usually the ones whose rate will change after you have committed.</p>
  <ul class="q-list">
    <li><strong>Material and class</strong> — PVC-U PN 10, or PE100 SDR 17 / PN 10. Not just "HDPE pipe".</li>
    <li><strong>Size basis</strong> — outside diameter in mm, and whether you are being quoted on OD or bore.</li>
    <li><strong>Length</strong> — 6 m lengths, 12 m lengths or coils, and whether cut lengths are available.</li>
    <li><strong>Fittings and jointing</strong> — are bends, tees, couplers and solvent cement included or extra?</li>
    <li><strong>Freight</strong> — delivered to site, or ex-plant? This is the most common hidden addition.</li>
    <li><strong>Validity and payment terms</strong> — polymer prices move, so a rate with no validity date is not a rate.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Where Al Qaryan Industries fits</h2>
  <p>We are a manufacturer, not a reseller. Al Qaryan Industries LLC extrudes PVC (uPVC) and HDPE (PE100) pipe at our own plant in the Shershah Industrial Area, Karachi, and supplies <strong>500 tons per month</strong> of each family with room to scale for scheduled programmes. The plant is ISO 9001 and ISO 14001 certified and pipe production is PSQCA licensed.</p>
  <p>We have traded plastics from Karachi since 2007, and the pipe lines sit beside our recycling division — so the compound is produced and controlled in-house rather than bought in and taken on trust. We also buy pipe scrap and offcuts back from fabricators and contractors, which keeps the material in the same loop.</p>
  <p>We do not publish a rate per metre, because the honest inputs are your size, your pressure class, your quantity and your delivery point. Send those four and you get a written price with a validity date, freight included and stated separately.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Mistakes that cost buyers the most</h2>
  <p>These come up repeatedly in enquiries, and every one of them is avoidable at the quotation stage.</p>
  <ul class="q-list">
    <li><strong>Comparing PN 6 with PN 10.</strong> Both are "PVC pipe"; one is half the wall. Check the class before the price.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring temperature.</strong> PVC-U is a cold-water product. Running it hot, or specifying it for a line that will see hot discharge, is a failure waiting to happen.</li>
    <li><strong>Buying on outside diameter alone.</strong> Two pipes of the same OD can have different wall thickness and pressure ratings.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting fittings in the budget.</strong> On short runs, fittings and jointing can be a meaningful share of the cost.</li>
    <li><strong>No written validity.</strong> Polymer prices move; a verbal rate from three weeks ago is not a quote.</li>
    <li><strong>Unmarked pipe.</strong> If the size, class and maker are not printed on the pipe, you cannot prove what you were sold.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Questions buyers ask before ordering</h2>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>How do I know if a pipe seller in Pakistan is a manufacturer?</summary><p>Ask which plant made the pipe and whether they will name it in writing, then ask for the PSQCA licence number and the plant address it belongs to. A manufacturer answers both immediately. A dealer may be perfectly legitimate, but you should know which one you are dealing with before you rely on them for a specification.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>What is the difference between PVC and uPVC pipe?</summary><p>They are the same product. uPVC stands for unplasticised polyvinyl chloride, written PVC-U in the standards. The "u" means no plasticiser was added, which is what makes rigid pressure pipe possible. It is not a grade, a brand or a premium version.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>What does SDR mean in HDPE pipe?</summary><p>SDR is the standard dimension ratio — the outside diameter divided by the wall thickness. A lower SDR means a thicker wall and a higher pressure rating: in PE100, SDR 17 is PN 10 and SDR 11 is PN 16. Quoting without the SDR is quoting without the pressure class.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Which is cheaper, PVC or HDPE pipe?</summary><p>It depends on size, pressure class, quantity and delivery point, and we will not pretend otherwise with a headline figure. As a general rule PVC-U is the more economical choice for cold-water and drainage work, while HDPE competes where flexibility, coiled lengths, trenchless installation or chemical resistance matter. Send the requirement and we will quote both so you can compare like for like.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Do you deliver outside Karachi?</summary><p>Yes. We deliver across Pakistan and confirm freight and lead time in the quotation. Give us the delivery location with the enquiry so the freight line is real rather than estimated.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Can you supply both PVC and HDPE on one order?</summary><p>Yes. Both families are extruded at the same plant, so a mixed order ships on one schedule with one point of accountability instead of two suppliers blaming each other.</p></details>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Related pages</h2>
  <div class="q-grid q-grid--3">
  <div class="q-card"><h3>PVC Pipes in Pakistan</h3><p>uPVC pressure classes, Schedule 40 and 80, and how to check a manufacturer.</p><a class="q-link" href="/pvc-pipes-pakistan/">PVC Pipes in Pakistan</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>HDPE 100 Pipes in Pakistan</h3><p>PE100 grades, SDR to PN ratings and supplier checks.</p><a class="q-link" href="/hdpe-pipes-pakistan/">HDPE 100 Pipes in Pakistan</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>PVC vs HDPE Pipe</h3><p>Which material to specify, and where each one fails.</p><a class="q-link" href="/pvc-vs-hdpe-pipes-pakistan/">PVC vs HDPE Pipe</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>Pipe Price Guide</h3><p>What actually drives the rate per metre in Pakistan.</p><a class="q-link" href="/pipe-price-guide-pakistan/">Pipe Price Guide</a></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="q-section q-cta">
  <h2>Send your requirement</h2>
  <p>Send the material or pipe size, the quantity and the delivery location. We confirm what we can supply, the price and the lead time — in writing, before you commit.</p>
  <p class="q-cta__actions"><a class="q-btn q-btn--primary" href="/contact/">Request a quote</a> <a class="q-btn q-btn--wa" href="https://wa.me/923332010506" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WhatsApp +92 333 201 0506</a></p>
  <p class="q-muted">Or call <a class="q-link" href="tel:+923332010506">+92 333 201 0506</a> — enquiries are answered within one working day.</p>
</div>

