  <item>
    <title><![CDATA[PVC and HDPE Pipe Price in Pakistan — What Drives the Rate]]></title>
    <link>https://alqaryangroup.com/pipe-price-guide-pakistan/</link>
    <pubDate>Sun, 01 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
    <dc:creator><![CDATA[qaryan_admin]]></dc:creator>
    <guid isPermaLink="false">https://alqaryangroup.com/?page_id=1107</guid>
    <description></description>
    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:html -->

<header class="q-site-head">
  <div class="q-site-head__inner">
    <a class="q-brand" href="/">
      <span class="q-brand__mark">AQ</span>
      <span class="q-brand__name">Al Qaryan <em>Industries</em></span>
    </a>
    <nav class="q-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href="/about/">About</a>
      <a href="/services/">Services</a>
      <a href="/materials-we-deal/">Materials We Deal</a>
      <a href="/we-buy/">We Buy Scrap</a>
      <a href="/facility/">Facility</a>
      <a href="/quality/">Quality</a>
      <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>
      <a class="q-nav__cta" href="/contact/">Contact</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
<div class="q-hero q-hero--compact">
  <p class="q-kicker">BUYING GUIDE · UNDERSTANDING PRICE</p>
  <h1>PVC and HDPE Pipe Price in Pakistan — What Drives the Rate</h1>
  <p class="q-lead">A published rate per metre for plastic pipe is out of date the moment polymer prices move, which is why we do not print one. What we can do is show you exactly which variables decide the number, so that two quotes become comparable and nothing appears after you commit.</p>
  <div class="q-chips"><span class="q-chip">No stale price list</span><span class="q-chip">SDR drives wall</span><span class="q-chip">Freight stated</span><span class="q-chip">Written validity</span><span class="q-chip">Karachi plant</span></div>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Why we do not publish a price list</h2>
  <p>Plastic pipe is priced off resin, and resin is a commodity that moves with oil, import parity and the rupee. A price list published this month is a complaint next month — either it is above the market and you overpay, or it is below and the seller quietly substitutes a thinner wall or a lower grade to protect the margin.</p>
  <p>The second reason is that "per metre" hides the only variable that matters: the pressure class. The same diameter in PN 6 and PN 16 is not the same product and not the same price. Any published single figure for "PVC pipe" is therefore either an assumption or a marketing number.</p>
  <p>What you should ask for instead is a written rate with the class, size, length and validity date printed on it. That is comparable. A headline figure is not.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>The seven variables that set your price</h2>
  <p>Every pipe quotation is a function of these. Change one and the number moves — which is also why two quotes that look different are often both correct.</p>
  <ul class="q-list">
    <li><strong>Material and grade.</strong> PE100 costs more than PE80 or PE63. In PVC, the compound and the additive package matter, and recycled content changes both price and the applications it is suitable for.</li>
    <li><strong>Pressure class.</strong> Lower SDR or higher PN means more wall, more material per metre. This is usually the biggest single driver.</li>
    <li><strong>Diameter.</strong> Material per metre rises with diameter faster than most buyers expect, because both the circumference and the wall increase.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantity.</strong> A single length and a full truckload are different transactions — one is picked from stock, the other is scheduled on the line.</li>
    <li><strong>Length and form.</strong> Standard 6 m or 12 m lengths versus cut lengths; coils versus straight lengths in HDPE. Coils cost more to produce and to freight.</li>
    <li><strong>Delivery distance.</strong> Plastic pipe is bulky and, in PVC's case, heavy. Karachi to Lahore is a real cost, not a rounding error.</li>
    <li><strong>Payment terms and validity.</strong> A rate held for 30 days carries a different risk to a rate held for 90. Say which you need.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Additives and colour are not decoration</h2>
  <p>For HDPE, carbon black is added for UV resistance and is normally specified at around two per cent by the standards — it is a performance requirement, not a colour choice, and pipe intended for outdoor exposure should state it.</p>
  <p>For PVC-U, the stabiliser system and any UV package affect both the price and how the pipe behaves in sun. Colour is usually functional: grey or white for water, other colours for specific services, and colour changes mean a separate production run at small quantities.</p>
  <p>If a quote is materially below others for the same stated class, the difference is usually in the compound, the additive package or the wall thickness. Ask which.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>How to compare two pipe quotes properly</h2>
  <p>Put both quotes side by side on these six lines. If a seller cannot fill them in, the comparison is not real yet.</p>
  <table class="q-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Material + class</th><td>PVC-U PN 10 / PE100 SDR 17 — not "PVC pipe"</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Size basis</th><td>Outside diameter in mm, stated explicitly</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Lengths + form</th><td>6 m, 12 m or coils; cut lengths or standard</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Fittings</th><td>Included or extra, and which jointing method</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Freight</th><td>Delivered or ex-plant, stated as its own line</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Validity</th><td>Number of days the rate holds</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="q-note">If one quote is dramatically cheaper, compare the pressure class line first. A one-step SDR difference usually explains the entire gap.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>What we need to quote you accurately</h2>
  <p>Four things, and we can answer the same day in most cases: the <strong>size</strong> (outside diameter), the <strong>pressure class or SDR</strong>, the <strong>quantity</strong> in metres or tons, and the <strong>delivery location</strong>. Add the application if you have it — buried main, irrigation, effluent, process line — because it sometimes changes the recommendation.</p>
  <p>Al Qaryan Industries manufactures both PVC (uPVC) and HDPE (PE100) pipe at our plant in the Shershah Industrial Area, Karachi, at <strong>500 tons per month</strong> of each family. Because we extrude rather than resell, the quote you receive is tied to our own line and our own compound, with a stated validity — not a rate passed down a chain.</p>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Questions buyers ask before ordering</h2>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Why is there no price list on your website?</summary><p>Because plastic pipe is priced off a moving commodity. A published rate goes stale within weeks and encourages sellers to protect margin by thinning the wall or downgrading the compound. We quote in writing against your size, pressure class, quantity and delivery point, with a validity date.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>What is the biggest factor in pipe price?</summary><p>The pressure class, because it sets the wall thickness and therefore how much material is in every metre. A lower SDR or higher PN class can move the price more than any other single variable. Diameter and quantity come next.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Does recycled content make pipe cheaper?</summary><p>It can reduce the material cost, but the application decides whether it is acceptable at all. Pressure pipe is specified against a standard and a class, and the compound must meet it. Ask any supplier to state the recycled content and the standard the pipe is made to, then decide whether that combination suits your duty.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>How quickly can you quote?</summary><p>Same day in most cases once we have the size, pressure class, quantity and delivery location. Add the application and we can also tell you if a different class would serve you better.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Do you quote delivered or ex-plant?</summary><p>Both, and the freight is shown as its own line so you can see what delivery costs. Give us the site location with the enquiry and the freight figure is real rather than a placeholder.</p></details>
  <details class="q-faq"><summary>Can I get both PVC and HDPE priced together?</summary><p>Yes. Both families are extruded at the same plant, so you receive one comparable sheet instead of two formats from two suppliers.</p></details>
</div>

<div class="q-section">
  <h2>Related pages</h2>
  <div class="q-grid q-grid--3">
  <div class="q-card"><h3>Pipe Sellers in Pakistan</h3><p>How to choose a seller and the six lines every quote needs.</p><a class="q-link" href="/pipe-sellers-pakistan/">Pipe Sellers in Pakistan</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>PVC vs HDPE Pipe</h3><p>Which material to specify for your duty.</p><a class="q-link" href="/pvc-vs-hdpe-pipes-pakistan/">PVC vs HDPE Pipe</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>PVC Pipes in Pakistan</h3><p>uPVC classes, Schedule 40 and 80.</p><a class="q-link" href="/pvc-pipes-pakistan/">PVC Pipes in Pakistan</a></div>
  <div class="q-card"><h3>HDPE 100 Pipes in Pakistan</h3><p>PE100 grades and SDR to PN.</p><a class="q-link" href="/hdpe-pipes-pakistan/">HDPE 100 Pipes in Pakistan</a></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="q-section q-cta">
  <h2>Send your requirement</h2>
  <p>Send the material or pipe size, the quantity and the delivery location. We confirm what we can supply, the price and the lead time — in writing, before you commit.</p>
  <p class="q-cta__actions"><a class="q-btn q-btn--primary" href="/contact/">Request a quote</a> <a class="q-btn q-btn--wa" href="https://wa.me/923332010506" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WhatsApp +92 333 201 0506</a></p>
  <p class="q-muted">Or call <a class="q-link" href="tel:+923332010506">+92 333 201 0506</a> — enquiries are answered within one working day.</p>
</div>

